 "api_error_status": null,
 "result": "Tagged JSON does it. Each non-JSON value is wrapped in a single-key dict whose key marks its type, and loading reverses that.\n\n- **`src/tessel/sessions.py`**:\n  - `SignedCookieSessionInterface` (line 284) sets `serializer = session_json_serializer` (line 300). That is a module-level `TypedCookieSerializer()` instance.\n  - `make_signer` passes the serializer to itsdangerous's `URLSafeTimedSerializer`, which signs the cookie. The session object itself is `SignedCookieSession`.\n- **`src/tessel/json/tag.py`**:\n  - `TypedCookieSerializer` (line 219) has `tag()`, `untag()`, `dumps()` and `loads()`.\n  - On dump, `tag()` tries each registered tagger in order. The first one whose check matches converts the value to a tagged form.\n  - On load, `untag()` looks at the single key of a dict, finds the matching tagger by that key, and rebuilds the original value.\n  - Each tagger subclasses `TypeMarker` (line 60). The built-in ones are:\n    - `TagDict` (escapes user dicts that look like tags)\n    - `PassDict`\n    - `TagTuple`\n    - `PassList`\n    - `TagBytes`\n    - `TagMarkup`\n    - `TagUUID`\n    - `TagDateTime`\n\nSo a tuple comes back as a tuple, bytes as bytes, and a datetime as a datetime. Plain dicts and lists pass through, with their contents tagged recursively. You can add your own type with `serializer.register(MyTag)`.\n\nI didn't open the bodies of the individual taggers, so I haven't checked the encodings they use.",
